   = note: its parameter is `t`
   = help: differentiate with respect to a parameter: `derivative(position, t)`

crates/sky-types/tests/ui/err_derivative_forms.sky:16:26: error: the second argument of `derivative` is the name of a parameter of `position`
   |
16 | b = derivative(position, 0)(1 s)
   |                          ^ not a parameter name
 3 | def position(t: s) -> m:
   |     -------- `position` is defined here
   = help: write the parameter's name, not its position: `derivative(position, t)`

crates/sky-types/tests/ui/err_derivative_forms.sky:17:23: error: cannot differentiate `loose`: its parameter `t` has no unit
   |
17 | c = derivative(loose, k)(1 s)
   |                       ^ while differentiating with respect to `k`
 6 | def loose(t, k: m) -> m:
   |     ----- `loose` is defined here
   = note: the derivative is computed through every parameter, so each needs a unit to type it
   = help: annotate the parameter with its unit, for example `t: s`

crates/sky-types/tests/ui/err_derivative_forms.sky:18:23: error: cannot differentiate `loose` with respect to `t`: it has no unit
   |
18 | d = derivative(loose, t)(1 s)
   |                       ^ `t` is not annotated
 6 | def loose(t, k: m) -> m:
   |     ----- `loose` is defined here
   = note: the derivative has the unit of the result divided by the unit of `t`, and an unannotated parameter accepts any unit
   = help: annotate the parameter with its unit, for example `t: s`

crates/sky-types/tests/ui/err_derivative_forms.sky:19:16: error: `derivative` needs a function defined with `def`, found the built-in `sqrt`
   |
19 | e = derivative(sqrt, x)(4)
   |                ^^^^ a built-in function
   = note: a `def` states the units of its parameters and result, which the unit of the derivative is computed from
   = help: wrap it in a `def` that states its units, and differentiate that

crates/sky-types/tests/ui/err_derivative_forms.sky:20:5: error: `derivative` takes 2 arguments, found 1: a function and the name of its parameter
   |
20 | f = derivative(position)(1 s)
   |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ missing argument
   = help: write `derivative(f, x)(...)`, then the arguments of `f`

crates/sky-types/tests/ui/err_derivative_forms.sky:21:16: error: `derivative` needs a function defined with `def`
   |
21 | g = derivative(3 m, t)(1 s)
   |                ^^^ this is length
   = help: define the function with `def f(x: unit) -> unit: ...` and differentiate that

crates/sky-types/tests/ui/err_derivative_forms.sky:22:22: error: cannot differentiate `norm` with respect to `v`: it is not a scalar
   |
22 | h = derivative(norm, v)(1 m)
   |                      ^ this is Vector[3] [L]
 9 | def norm(v: Vector[3] m) -> m:
   |             ----------- declared here
   = note: the derivative with respect to an array is a gradient, which comes with arrays at run time
   = help: differentiate with respect to a scalar parameter

crates/sky-types/tests/ui/err_derivative_forms.sky:23:16: error: cannot differentiate `path`: its result is Vector[3] [L], not a number
   |
23 | i = derivative(path, t)(1 s)
   |                ^^^^ this function's result
12 | def path(t: s) -> Vector[3] m:
   |     ---- `path` is defined here
   = note: the derivative of an array-valued function is a Jacobian, which comes with arrays at run time

crates/sky-types/tests/ui/err_derivative_forms.sky:24:16: error: `derivative` needs a function defined with `def`, found another derivative
   |
24 | j = derivative(derivative(position, t), t)(1 s)
   |                ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ this is `derivative(...)`, which can only be called
   = help: name the first derivative with a `def` that calls it, and differentiate that

crates/sky-types/tests/ui/err_derivative_forms.sky:25:5: error: `derivative(position, t)` is a function, and must be called where it is written
   |
25 | k = derivative(position, t)
   |     ^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^^ not called
   = note: Sky has no function values yet: a function can be called, but not stored or passed around
   = help: pass the arguments of the function: `derivative(position, t)(...)`, or name the derivative with a `def` that calls it
